The statement of cash flows is divided into three separate activities: operating
activities, investing activities, and financing activities. These activities, which are
explained in the following list, are the activities from which a firm obtains and
uses cash:

@ Operating activities: Operating activities include net income (or loss),
depreciation, and changes in current assets and current liabilities other
than cash and short-term debt. A firm’s net income, taken from its
income statement, is the first line on the corresponding period’s cash flow
statement.

